# React Component Conventions

## Inline Prop Types

Define component prop types inline in the function parameter instead of creating a separate `interface` or `type` alias.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD
interface ButtonProps {
  label: string;
  onClick: () => void;
}

export function Button({ label, onClick }: ButtonProps) {
  return (
    <button type="button" onClick={onClick}>
      {label}
    </button>
  );
}

// ✅ GOOD
export default function Button({
  label,
  onClick,
}: {
  label: string;
  onClick: () => void;
}) {
  return (
    <button type="button" onClick={onClick}>
      {label}
    </button>
  );
}
```

Prefer this pattern for all React components in this project unless a shared external contract is explicitly required.

## Default Export for Main Component

The primary component of a file should be declared as `export default function`. Secondary/helper components in the same file use named exports.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD
export function ProfileScreen() {
  return <div>...</div>;
}

// ❌ BAD
const ProfileScreen = () => {
  return <div>...</div>;
};
export default ProfileScreen;

// ✅ GOOD
export default function ProfileScreen() {
  return <div>...</div>;
}
```
